1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,388
Right now, Earth's spin
carries you at up to

2
00:00:03,388 --> 00:00:07,726
1,670 kilometers per hour,
and you can't feel it.

3
00:00:08,126 --> 00:00:09,866
So what if, for one second,

4
00:00:09,866 --> 00:00:12,476
the ground under you stopped,
and everything else didn't?

5
00:00:13,776 --> 00:00:16,861
Welcome to The If Engine,
where we run impossible

6
00:00:16,861 --> 00:00:19,945
scenarios through real
physics, and show you the math.

7
00:00:20,345 --> 00:00:26,300
First, the speed. Earth turns once
every 23.93 hours relative to the stars.

8
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:30,022
That's the sidereal day, about
four minutes shorter than the

9
00:00:30,022 --> 00:00:34,861
24-hour day on your clock, because
Earth is also circling the Sun.

10
00:00:35,261 --> 00:00:38,475
Divide the distance around
the equator by that time,

11
00:00:38,475 --> 00:00:42,403
and you get about 465 meters per second.

12
00:00:42,403 --> 00:00:46,331
That's roughly 1,670 kilometers per hour,

13
00:00:46,331 --> 00:00:48,474
faster than the speed of sound.

14
00:00:48,874 --> 00:00:50,806
But not everyone moves that fast.

15
00:00:50,806 --> 00:00:53,060
The farther you are from the equator,

16
00:00:53,060 --> 00:00:55,636
the smaller the circle
you trace every day.

17
00:00:56,036 --> 00:01:02,606
In Singapore, you're doing about 465
meters per second. In Cairo, 403.

18
00:01:02,606 --> 00:01:06,622
New York, 353. London, 290.

19
00:01:06,622 --> 00:01:10,272
And at the North Pole,
you're just turning in place.

20
00:01:10,672 --> 00:01:14,156
We never feel any of this,
because the ground, the air,

21
00:01:14,156 --> 00:01:17,958
the oceans and you are all moving
together, at a steady speed.

22
00:01:17,958 --> 00:01:21,759
Motion you share with everything
around you is motion you can't feel.

23
00:01:22,159 --> 00:01:24,678
Which is exactly why
breaking that agreement,

24
00:01:24,678 --> 00:01:27,917
even for one second, is
such a terrible idea.

25
00:01:29,217 --> 00:01:31,444
Here are the rules of today's experiment.

26
00:01:31,844 --> 00:01:33,848
Rule one: the solid Earth,

27
00:01:33,848 --> 00:01:37,856
every rock and everything bolted
to it, stops rotating instantly.

28
00:01:38,256 --> 00:01:41,943
Rule two: it stays perfectly
still for exactly one second.

29
00:01:42,343 --> 00:01:46,633
Rule three: it snaps back to its
normal spin, as if nothing happened.

30
00:01:47,033 --> 00:01:49,699
Everything that isn't
rock, the air, the water,

31
00:01:49,699 --> 00:01:52,031
and you, simply follows
Newton's first law.

32
00:01:52,031 --> 00:01:55,030
It keeps moving at the
speed it already had.

33
00:01:56,330 --> 00:01:57,709
Let's start the clock.

34
00:01:57,709 --> 00:01:59,777
At time zero, the ground stops.

35
00:01:59,777 --> 00:02:01,845
Say you're standing on the equator.

36
00:02:01,845 --> 00:02:03,914
From the ground's point of view,

37
00:02:03,914 --> 00:02:08,050
you're suddenly hurtling east
at 465 meters per second.

38
00:02:08,450 --> 00:02:09,849
Can friction save you?

39
00:02:09,849 --> 00:02:12,647
Rubber on dry concrete can slow you down

40
00:02:12,647 --> 00:02:15,795
at about 6.9 meters per second squared.

41
00:02:15,795 --> 00:02:19,993
Over the whole second, that takes
off just 7 meters per second.

42
00:02:19,993 --> 00:02:22,791
You'd still be going 458.

43
00:02:23,191 --> 00:02:25,017
If nothing were in your way,

44
00:02:25,017 --> 00:02:28,061
you'd tumble almost half a
kilometer in that one second.

45
00:02:28,461 --> 00:02:30,571
But on a planet covered in walls,

46
00:02:30,571 --> 00:02:33,284
trees and cars, something
is always in your way.

47
00:02:33,284 --> 00:02:35,092
So how hard is that hit?

48
00:02:35,492 --> 00:02:40,429
At 465 meters per second,
a 70 kilogram person

49
00:02:40,429 --> 00:02:44,131
carries about 7.6 megajoules
of kinetic energy.

50
00:02:44,131 --> 00:02:49,068
That's the energy released by
roughly 1.8 kilograms of TNT.

51
00:02:49,468 --> 00:02:51,724
Here's another way to feel that number.

52
00:02:51,724 --> 00:02:55,270
Hitting a wall at that
speed is like falling from a

53
00:02:55,270 --> 00:02:58,815
height of 11 kilometers,
with no air to slow you down.

54
00:02:58,815 --> 00:03:00,427
That's higher than Mount Everest.

55
00:03:00,827 --> 00:03:04,351
In London, the numbers are
smaller, but not small enough.

56
00:03:04,351 --> 00:03:07,523
Even at 290 meters per second,

57
00:03:07,523 --> 00:03:11,047
it's the same as falling
from 4.3 kilometers.

58
00:03:12,347 --> 00:03:15,492
Now, buildings. Unlike you,
they're anchored to the rock.

59
00:03:15,492 --> 00:03:19,336
So when the rock stops, their
foundations stop with it, instantly.

60
00:03:19,736 --> 00:03:21,953
The upper floors don't know that yet.

61
00:03:21,953 --> 00:03:25,119
The news travels up the
structure as a shear wave,

62
00:03:25,119 --> 00:03:27,336
a kind of sound wave in steel,

63
00:03:27,336 --> 00:03:30,503
moving at about 3,200 meters per second.

64
00:03:30,903 --> 00:03:33,867
The stress that wave carries is density,

65
00:03:33,867 --> 00:03:37,677
times wave speed, times the
sudden change in velocity.

66
00:03:37,677 --> 00:03:40,217
For steel, that's about 12 gigapascals.

67
00:03:40,617 --> 00:03:42,734
Common structural steel starts to give

68
00:03:42,734 --> 00:03:46,262
way in shear at around 140 megapascals.

69
00:03:46,262 --> 00:03:49,084
Our stress wave is about 80 times that.

70
00:03:49,084 --> 00:03:53,670
Structures bolted to the ground just
aren't built for a jolt like this.

71
00:03:54,070 --> 00:03:57,818
And yet, as bad as all that is,
it isn't the biggest problem.

72
00:03:57,818 --> 00:04:00,413
The biggest problem is
the thing you can't see.

73
00:04:01,713 --> 00:04:03,841
The atmosphere isn't bolted to anything.

74
00:04:03,841 --> 00:04:06,679
When the ground stops,
the air keeps going.

75
00:04:06,679 --> 00:04:11,645
At the equator, that's a wind
of 465 meters per second,

76
00:04:11,645 --> 00:04:13,418
blowing from west to east.

77
00:04:13,818 --> 00:04:19,143
At sea level, sound travels at
about 340 meters per second.

78
00:04:19,143 --> 00:04:23,708
So at the equator, this wind is Mach 1.37.

79
00:04:23,708 --> 00:04:27,892
It stays supersonic everywhere within
about 43 degrees of the equator.

80
00:04:27,892 --> 00:04:30,935
That's two-thirds of Earth's
surface, all at once.

81
00:04:31,335 --> 00:04:34,844
Anything standing in its way
gets pushed with at least

82
00:04:34,844 --> 00:04:38,002
half the air density times
the wind speed squared.

83
00:04:38,002 --> 00:04:40,107
That's 132 kilopascals,

84
00:04:40,107 --> 00:04:43,967
about 14 tonnes of force on
every square meter of wall.

85
00:04:44,367 --> 00:04:50,103
For comparison, a Category 5 hurricane
starts at 252 kilometers per hour.

86
00:04:50,103 --> 00:04:53,544
That push grows with
the square of the speed,

87
00:04:53,544 --> 00:04:58,898
so our wind pushes about 44 times
harder than the minimum Category 5 wind.

88
00:04:59,298 --> 00:05:04,011
Across that zone, every building, tree
and hill would grow its own shock wave.

89
00:05:04,011 --> 00:05:07,377
Picture a sonic boom, but
instead of one passing jet,

90
00:05:07,377 --> 00:05:10,407
it comes from every
obstacle at the same moment.

91
00:05:10,807 --> 00:05:16,572
Altogether, the moving air
carries about 3.7 × 10²³

92
00:05:16,572 --> 00:05:19,865
joules of kinetic energy
relative to the ground.

93
00:05:19,865 --> 00:05:23,983
That's roughly 600 years of
humanity's entire energy use.

94
00:05:24,383 --> 00:05:25,687
But here's the twist.

95
00:05:25,687 --> 00:05:28,294
It only has one second to deliver it.

96
00:05:28,294 --> 00:05:32,532
In one second, even the fastest air
moves less than half a kilometer,

97
00:05:32,532 --> 00:05:35,791
and most of the air above
the rooftops never touches

98
00:05:35,791 --> 00:05:38,073
anything solid before
the ground starts again.

99
00:05:39,373 --> 00:05:44,799
Now, the oceans. Like the air, the water
keeps moving while the sea floor stops.

100
00:05:44,799 --> 00:05:48,778
The open ocean is, on
average, 3.7 kilometers deep,

101
00:05:48,778 --> 00:05:51,310
and near the equator that entire column

102
00:05:51,310 --> 00:05:55,651
of water slides east by up to 465 meters.

103
00:05:56,051 --> 00:06:00,347
That's about 1.7 million cubic
meters of water shoved toward

104
00:06:00,347 --> 00:06:04,286
the shore for every single
meter of coastline in its path.

105
00:06:04,286 --> 00:06:07,866
Roughly 690 Olympic
swimming pools, per meter.

106
00:06:08,266 --> 00:06:10,985
But the coasts are where it gets violent.

107
00:06:10,985 --> 00:06:14,045
When fast-moving water slams
into a steep rock face,

108
00:06:14,045 --> 00:06:16,424
it can't get out of the way.

109
00:06:16,424 --> 00:06:20,164
Engineers call this water hammer,
and the pressure spike is density,

110
00:06:20,164 --> 00:06:23,903
times the speed of sound in
water, times the impact speed.

111
00:06:24,303 --> 00:06:28,639
With water hitting rock
at 465 meters per second,

112
00:06:28,639 --> 00:06:31,169
that's around 720 megapascals,

113
00:06:31,169 --> 00:06:36,228
more than 6 times the crushing pressure
at the bottom of the Mariana Trench.

114
00:06:36,628 --> 00:06:40,347
On the coasts facing the other
way, the water does the reverse.

115
00:06:40,347 --> 00:06:42,206
It pulls away from the land,

116
00:06:42,206 --> 00:06:45,305
for a moment uncovering the
sea floor near the shore.

117
00:06:45,705 --> 00:06:50,084
Once the ground starts again, gravity
tries to level everything out,

118
00:06:50,084 --> 00:06:52,871
and that sends tsunamis
across every ocean.

119
00:06:52,871 --> 00:06:55,657
In water 3.7 kilometers deep,

120
00:06:55,657 --> 00:07:00,434
a tsunami travels at about
684 kilometers per hour,

121
00:07:00,434 --> 00:07:03,221
nearly as fast as a jet airliner,

122
00:07:03,221 --> 00:07:07,998
crossing 5,000 kilometers
of ocean in about 7.3 hours.

123
00:07:08,398 --> 00:07:10,506
How tall would those waves be?

124
00:07:10,506 --> 00:07:14,722
Honestly, that depends on the shape
of every coastline and sea floor.

125
00:07:14,722 --> 00:07:17,181
That takes a supercomputer, not a video.

126
00:07:17,181 --> 00:07:21,748
But the push is enormous, and it
reaches every ocean basin at once.

127
00:07:23,048 --> 00:07:24,670
So, is anyone safe?

128
00:07:24,670 --> 00:07:25,481
Surprisingly, yes.

129
00:07:25,481 --> 00:07:27,914
Anyone who isn't touching the ground.

130
00:07:28,314 --> 00:07:31,634
A cruising airliner is
carried by the air around it,

131
00:07:31,634 --> 00:07:33,294
and that air doesn't stop.

132
00:07:33,294 --> 00:07:36,614
For that one second, passengers
would feel nothing at all,

133
00:07:36,614 --> 00:07:39,934
while the ground far below
them lurches to a halt.

134
00:07:40,334 --> 00:07:43,614
And the astronauts on the
International Space Station,

135
00:07:43,614 --> 00:07:48,124
orbiting at about 28,000 kilometers
per hour, wouldn't notice anything.

136
00:07:48,124 --> 00:07:52,634
Their orbit doesn't care how fast
the planet underneath is spinning.

137
00:07:53,934 --> 00:07:55,307
Then the second is up.

138
00:07:55,307 --> 00:07:57,229
The rock snaps back to full speed.

139
00:07:57,629 --> 00:08:00,083
That should fix everything,
right? Not quite.

140
00:08:00,083 --> 00:08:04,289
Anything that got slowed down during
that second, by hitting a wall,

141
00:08:04,289 --> 00:08:07,795
scraping along the ground or
piling up against a coast,

142
00:08:07,795 --> 00:08:10,599
is now moving slower
than the ground again.

143
00:08:10,999 --> 00:08:13,396
So the jolt happens again, in reverse.

144
00:08:13,396 --> 00:08:16,820
Everything that was stopped gets
hit by the restarting planet

145
00:08:16,820 --> 00:08:21,614
at up to 465 meters per second,
from the other direction.

146
00:08:22,014 --> 00:08:23,644
Two hits, one second apart.

147
00:08:23,644 --> 00:08:26,579
That's the real price of
a single stopped second.

148
00:08:27,879 --> 00:08:31,556
Once the dust settles, there's
one strange, permanent change.

149
00:08:31,556 --> 00:08:34,824
Earth has lost exactly
one second of rotation.

150
00:08:35,224 --> 00:08:39,653
In that second, the planet should have
turned by about 15 arcseconds. It didn't.

151
00:08:39,653 --> 00:08:42,501
So every place on Earth is now one second

152
00:08:42,501 --> 00:08:45,032
behind where the stars say it should be.

153
00:08:45,432 --> 00:08:48,231
Astronomers track exactly
this kind of drift.

154
00:08:48,231 --> 00:08:50,230
Atomic clocks keep steady time,

155
00:08:50,230 --> 00:08:54,228
but Earth's spin isn't
perfectly steady, so since 1972,

156
00:08:54,228 --> 00:08:56,627
timekeepers have inserted 27 leap seconds

157
00:08:56,627 --> 00:09:00,225
to keep our clocks in
step with the planet.

158
00:09:00,625 --> 00:09:03,541
So after all that destruction,
the official cleanup

159
00:09:03,541 --> 00:09:07,187
would include one tiny line
item: one more leap second.

160
00:09:08,487 --> 00:09:11,701
So, what if Earth stopped
spinning for one second?

161
00:09:11,701 --> 00:09:14,915
A supersonic wind, a stress
wave through every building,

162
00:09:14,915 --> 00:09:16,701
water hammer on the coasts,

163
00:09:16,701 --> 00:09:20,986
tsunamis in every ocean, and then
the whole thing again in reverse.

164
00:09:21,386 --> 00:09:22,932
One second is short.

165
00:09:22,932 --> 00:09:24,091
Momentum doesn't care.

166
00:09:24,491 --> 00:09:28,582
Every number in this video comes
from a calculation or a cited source,

167
00:09:28,582 --> 00:09:32,987
and the sources are in the description,
so go ahead and check our math.

168
00:09:33,387 --> 00:09:36,169
Next time, we leave solid
ground behind entirely.

169
00:09:36,169 --> 00:09:38,603
What happens if you fall into Jupiter,

170
00:09:38,603 --> 00:09:41,733
a planet with no solid surface to land on?

171
00:09:41,733 --> 00:09:43,820
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

172
00:09:43,820 --> 00:09:45,558
This was The If Engine.

173
00:09:45,558 --> 00:09:47,645
Run the impossible. Do the math.
